do you want to see my new favorite shape  yeah of course you do it's this one here  it's called the largest small hexagon  and i just love it because i love its  name the largest small hexagon so let's  break down what the name means so it's a  hexagon which is a six sided shape small  means that all the diagonals like corner  to corner they're all less than and  equal to one that's what small means and  then largest small hexagon means it has  the largest area with that rule if all  the diagonals are less than or equal to  one the idea is it could fit  in a  one by one box like that  in fact you could roll it around inside  that box and it would fit nicely in  there  what's surprising is it's not the  regular hexagon because that's what my  guess would have been okay what is the  largest area if you had all the  diagonals fitting inside a one by one  box and i would have guessed regular  hexagon  but it's not so it's this thing so this  is not a regular hexagon it's a regular  it has a larger area than the regular  hexagon by about four percent and this  was discovered in the 1970s by ron  graham so let me show you a little bit  more about this so i was surprised that  it wasn't a regular hexagon and if we  did um shapes with less sides it  actually is the regular shape  so for a triangle  the largest small triangle  is  the regular equilateral triangle these  have a side length one  and that would be your answer  for a quadrilateral  it is the square the square has  diagonals of one so it's not side length  one it's diagonals of length one that is  your largest small quad draw lateral  although there's more than one answer  for the quadrilateral because it could  be  any quadrilateral which has two  diagonals that are at right angles to  each other that have length one this has  length one and this has length one like  that if i put them like that that would  be a square but i could have a  quadrilateral of any sort of kite shape  like this and those would all have the  same answer they all have the same area  so they all count as the largest small  quadrilateral if you're looking at the  pentagon it's the regular pentagon so  here is the regular pentagon and then  your diagonals have length one and that  is the largest small  pentagon so therefore you think ah the  largest small hexagon  would be the regular hexagon  here it is but it turns out  it's not  so to solve this what ron grain did is  he used some graph theory which is a  little bit surprising graph theory is  the maths of points and networks so dots  and lines and he knew that some of these  diagonals had to have length one and he  just kind of looked at all the  possibilities and he drew them as graphs  he knew that some of these possibilities  were definitely not going to be the  largest area so you could get rid of  those and he had about  10 possibilities to go through and he  got rid of them he says no that doesn't  work and then with a few that he had  left he sort of wiggled the angles and  said well if i wiggle the angle does  that make it bit larger he worked on it  a bit longer and then he found  this shape so here it is again so this  is our  largest small hexagon but there is some  symmetry it's not the regular hexagon  but there is some symmetry to this it  looks like a pentagon it does look like  a pencil there's a kind kind of a reason  why it looks like a pentagon let me show  you so the way it's being constructed  actually is it's this shape this is a  pentagon where all the diagonals are  equal to one and then for the hexagon  all we've done is we've taken a vertical  line down here of length one and we've  added an extra corner to the bottom of  this pentagon so that's kind of the  shape  of the largest small hexagon so then ron  gray and said are the other  shapes that have even number side is  that the same kind of idea for that that  was his conjecture  well we didn't prove that for a long  time the octagon took another 30 years  to prove  and we did we proved it  so the octagon isn't the regular octagon  but it is this shape here here we have  our largest small octagon and it was  made the same way so started with a  seven-sided shape like this all the  diagonals have length one add on one  more diagonal and a little extra corner  to the bottom there and that's why we  get our octagon and then only recently  2007 we we were able to prove that this  is true so for the even sided shapes  this is how you construct the largest  small  shape want even more hexagons we'll go  get them at brilliant today's episode  sponsor  brilliance full of courses quizzes and  problems to sharpen your brain to lift  your game  everything's interactive as you can see  here and designed with loving care  it's made to make you smarter but also  put a smile on your face  whether you're a beginner or a veteran a  school student or a lifelong learner  brilliant has more than enough stuff to  keep you busy  already a subscriber then why not give  brilliant as a gift  by the way get 20 off a premium  all-inclusive subscription by going to  brilliant.org  number five details and a link can be  found down in the video description  [Music]
 

